# Task list

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A task list breaks a longer setup into named tasks and shows how far along each one is. Use it when people may finish the work in several sessions or in an order they choose, and when completed work must be kept.

Do not use it for a short two-step change, for a list of saved answers or for live device readings. Use a [Summary list](summary-list.html) for answers, and show readings as ordinary state.

Home Assistant has no task list element, card or theme setting. This is a Civica pattern. A dashboard can draw a plain version of it, as described below. The Home Assistant to-do card is a different thing: it holds items people add, edit and tick off. See [To-do lists, reminders and household tasks](../home-assistant/todo-and-reminders.html).

This page covers:

- Civica task list pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A task list is a heading, a line saying how many tasks are done, and one row for each task.

1. A heading that names the setup, such as “Set up household notifications”.
2. A progress line in words, such as “1 of 3 tasks completed”.
3. One row for each task. A row has a task name, an optional hint and a status.
4. The task name is a link when the task can be started. It is plain text when it cannot.
5. The status is written as text beside the name. A short boxed label can sit in the status, such as “There is a problem”.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-task-list-pattern">Civica task list pattern</h3>

A Civica pattern for a list of tasks with a written status. Home Assistant has no element of this name, so a dashboard cannot place one directly.

**What a dashboard can build.** A [Markdown card](markdown-card.html) can show a plain list. Lists, links and bold text are kept. Classes, styles and ids are removed, so the status cannot be styled and cannot be tied to its link. Write the status in the same line as the task name:

```markdown
- [Choose who receives notifications](/config/helpers): Completed
- [Select electricity meter](/config/energy): There is a problem. The saved meter was removed.
- Review and turn on notifications: Cannot start yet. Finish the other tasks first.
```

For items that people add, edit and tick off, use the to-do card (`type: todo-list`) instead.

**Statuses.** Use a small, fixed set of words, and use the same words every time:

| Status | Use it when |
| --- | --- |
| Not yet started | The task can be started and nothing has been saved |
| In progress | Some work is saved but the task is not finished |
| Completed | All the work the task needs is saved |
| There is a problem | Something saved earlier no longer works, and the row says why |
| Cannot start yet | A task it depends on is not finished, and the row says which |

**What the Civica theme sets.** The theme has no setting for this pattern. Text uses the primary text colour, hints use the secondary text colour and links use the link colour. Colour can help people see the status, but the words always carry it.

**What stays as Home Assistant draws it.** Anything drawn inside a Markdown card keeps the Markdown card’s own spacing and fonts.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- People can leave and come back, because each task has its own state.
- Each status is written as text, so it never depends on colour.
- A dependency is explained in words instead of a link that does nothing.
- A Markdown card cannot link a status to its task or style it, so the status sits in the same line as the name.
- Nothing updates the list by itself. The page that holds it must change the wording when the work is saved.
- Do not rely on a screen reader announcing a changed status. Say in the page text what changed.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Mark a task completed only when its required work is saved. Explain dependencies in plain text: “Finish the other tasks first” tells people what to do next, and a greyed-out link does not. Keep any final turn-on step separate from progress, so finishing the list does not switch anything on by itself.

Give each task one clear link. Do not put a second link inside a linked row. Start task names with a verb, keep them in the same order every time and keep the same wording for the same task on every page. Say how many tasks are done in words, such as “1 of 3 tasks completed”. Let people leave and resume, keep the answers they gave and do not move them on automatically.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: write the rows as a list, and keep each status next to its task.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: write the status in words. “Cannot start yet” must be readable without colour.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context): the task name says where the link goes.
- 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation and 3.2.4 Consistent Identification: keep the order and the names the same.
- 3.3.7 Redundant Entry: keep earlier answers when people return to a task.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: when a status changes after a save, say so in the page text.

